---
import RunCountdown from "../../../components/studies/RunCountdown.svelte";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find(
	(entry) => entry.slug === "run-countdown-2026-11-05",
);
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
	<section>
		<h2>01 / Three seconds before control</h2>
		<p>
			Pressing Launch now cross-fades from the menu to flight in 0.3 seconds,
			and the ship is live at once. Runs are short and challenging, and their
			first moments decide orientation: where the planet is, which way the ship
			is drifting. A countdown of 3, 2, 1 and then GO or 0 would give each run a
			deliberate threshold. The proposal is to paint it as light on the effects
			surface rather than as HUD text, in Android green or another ink.
		</p>
		<p>
			The count has to read instantly while it shares the screen with the ship
			it is about to release, and it costs three seconds of every run. On the
			effects surface a numeral is stamped light: overlap adds, and the shared
			game-time decay fades it. The afterglow and the change between digits can
			therefore come from the surface itself, with stencil cuts, saturation and
			flicker as the expressive means. Ink also carries a role: lime is the
			brand and marks what can be acted on, Android green is a status colour,
			and the effects inks are transient light.
		</p>
		<p>
			This cycle aims to settle how the countdown looks and moves at gameplay
			scale: typeface, ink, the motion of each digit, the final mark and its
			place relative to the ship. Whether the world moves during the count and
			when input becomes live are open; the examples assume a still scene with
			control at the final mark. Restarts, audio and reduced motion are adjacent
			questions outside this cycle.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section>
		<h2 id="light-numerals">02.1 / Light numerals on the effects surface</h2>
		<p>
			<strong
				>Accepted: B / Ignition in Saira Stencil and Android green, centred on
				the ship, without a launch ring.</strong
			>
			The demo opens on this variant; the other switches remain for comparison.
			Settled properties: the bold stencil face, the Android green ink with a
			hot core, the flickering strike at each digit's onset, and the count
			sitting over the ship at full size. Whether the count ends on GO or 0 is
			still open; the reference uses GO.
		</p>
		<figure class="reference">
			<img
				src="/studies/run-countdown-2026-11-05/02-1-ignition-strike.png"
				alt="Digit 2 mid-strike: the green stencil numeral slipped sideways during its flicker, over the Wasp and starfield"
				width="1056"
				height="595"
			>
			<img
				src="/studies/run-countdown-2026-11-05/02-1-ignition-steady.png"
				alt="Digit 2 steady: the green stencil numeral with a soft halo, centred over the Wasp"
				width="1056"
				height="595"
			>
			<figcaption>
				Accepted reference, captured from the demo at 1280 px. Conditions: a
				1920 × 1080 backing canvas, still camera, no randomness. 0.6 s lead,
				then one digit per second. Each digit is stamped for 0.8 s at 0.45
				intensity; the strike lasts 0.28 s, with a deterministic on/off/half
				pattern every 1/30 s and an 18 px slip on half steps. The final mark is
				stamped for 0.4 s at 0.6. The glyph is 420 px at that backing, cached
				with a 48 px halo, an ink body and a 30% core.
			</figcaption>
		</figure>
		<p>
			Each motion variant paints through the production <code>FxSurface</code>:
			a cached luminous glyph (halo, ink body, hot core) is stamped each frame
			while a digit is active, and the shared decay does the rest. A tests the
			bare surface fade, B a cheap neon strike, C motion that the persistent
			image turns into an echo. The final mark can release a launch ring. Ink,
			typeface, final mark, placement and backdrop are independent switches.
		</p>
		<p>
			The scene is a still composition: the starfield and Wasp come from the
			production renderer, but no simulation runs and the camera does not move.
			The example cannot show whether three seconds feels long across repeated
			runs; that needs a flight trial. Saira Stencil is loaded only on this
			page. Production would need only the glyphs 0–3, G and O, which could ship
			as outlines, as the logomark does, instead of a font file.
		</p>
		<RunCountdown client:load />
	</section>
	<section>
		<h2>03 / What the start flow already gives</h2>
		<p>
			Launch already has a starting phase in <code>FlightDeck</code>: a 150 ms
			fade to void, the handoff to a fresh survival run, then a 150 ms reveal.
			Input, resources and the simulation clock wait for the reveal. The
			countdown extends that wait by three seconds, with the revealed run on
			screen and still. Flight Lab and builder flights skip the starting phase
			and stay unaffected. <code>/lab/landing</code> already composes the
			production launcher and handoff, so it shows the countdown with no new
			Lab page.
		</p>
		<p>
			The shared surface is ready for the effect: additive stamping, a cached
			luminous stamp built once with its halo, and the essential budget for a
			cue that must not drop. One adaptation is needed. <code>FlightFx</code>
			advances the surface with simulation ticks, which stay at zero during
			the count, so the light would neither fade nor accept new stamps. The
			surface needs a presentation clock: count time during the countdown,
			then count time plus ticks, which stays monotonic. Stamping the digits in
			world space at the ship's starting position, scaled by the inverse zoom,
			keeps their screen size. It also lets the last light stay where the ship
			launched once the camera starts following, as painted light already
			does.
		</p>
		<p>
			The face ships as outlines rather than a font file. The countdown needs
			only 0–3, G and O, and <code>scripts/convert-logomark.py</code> already
			turns OFL glyphs into committed paths. A Path2D stamp cache replaces the
			study's woff2. The countdown is HUD information, the status of a run about
			to start, so it keeps the <code>--color-android-green</code> token; the
			effects surface is only its painter. The canvas mirror in
			<code>packages/core/src/lib/ui/colors.ts</code> does not yet carry that
			token, so it gains one under the existing parity check. The countdown is
			the first canvas text outside the <code>ui</code> face, so the typeface
			table in the design-system core gains a row.
		</p>
		<p>
			Unresolved choices, provisional for delivery: the final mark (GO in the
			reference); control going live with the final mark, and whether a thrust
			key held through the count fires at once (provisionally yes); a hidden
			page or blur pausing the count, matching the handoff; and reduced motion
			keeping its immediate start, with no countdown. None of these blocks
			delivery.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section>
		<h2>04 / Two slices to a counted start</h2>
		<p>
			<strong>No <code>docs/design.md</code> change.</strong>
			The countdown adds a canvas mirror of an existing colour token and a
			typeface row in the design-system core spec; experience principles, art
			direction and brand stay as they are.
		</p>
		<h3>S1 — Countdown glyphs and ignition painter (core, small–medium)</h3>
		<ul>
			<li>
				Generate Saira Stencil 700 outlines for 0–3, G and O with the
				logomark's conversion script; commit them with the OFL licence.
			</li>
			<li>
				Mirror <code>--color-android-green</code> in the canvas
				<code>COLOR</code> map, kept equal by
				<code>scripts/check-contracts.mjs</code>. Add a painter in
				<code>packages/core/src/lib/effects/</code> that maps count time and
				final mark to cached luminous stamps in that colour, using the accepted
				strike, hold and release.
			</li>
			<li>
				Specs: <code>docs/specs/sfx-canvas.md</code> (countdown emitter) and
				<code>docs/specs/design-system-core.md</code> (typeface row).
			</li>
			<li>
				Correctness: digits appear in descending order and then the final mark;
				nothing is stamped outside the active windows; equal time gives equal
				strike output; stamps use the essential allowance.
			</li>
			<li>
				Fidelity: frames match the 02.1 reference conditions above.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<h3>S2 — Counted start in play (web and mode, medium; needs S1)</h3>
		<ul>
			<li>
				Extend the starting phase in <code>FlightDeck</code> with a countdown
				phase after the reveal, exposed as <code>data-phase</code>. The run's
				simulation stays at zero ticks until the final mark, and it starts
				with the mark.
			</li>
			<li>
				Give <code>FlightFx</code> a presentation clock and let the countdown
				stamp into the shared surface at the ship's world position.
			</li>
			<li>
				Spec: the landing section of <code>docs/specs/playable-alpha.md</code>
				records the counted start and the provisional choices from 03.
			</li>
			<li>
				Correctness, in <code>tests/browser/landing.spec.ts</code> and unit
				tests: Launch enters the countdown, and ticks and resources stay
				unchanged through it. Input delivered during the count does not move
				the ship; play begins at the final mark. Repeated activation is
				ignored, and a hidden page pauses the count. Reduced motion starts
				immediately, and Lab and builder flights are unchanged. The surface
				decays during the count and keeps the countdown's light at its world
				position after play begins.
			</li>
			<li>
				Fidelity: <code>/lab/landing</code> shows the accepted reference over
				the real run at 1280 px.
			</li>
		</ul>
	</section>
</StudyLayout>
<style>
.reference {
	display: grid;
	grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
	gap: var(--spacing-sm);
	margin: var(--spacing-lg) 0;
}
.reference img {
	width: 100%;
	height: auto;
	border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
}
.reference figcaption {
	grid-column: 1 / -1;
	color: var(--color-text-muted);
	font: var(--font-size-caption) var(--font-family-ui);
}
</style>
